## MUST

**M1 — class: the manifest validator's clause ordering is unspecified, and
three behaviour rows assert exact finding counts that depend on it.**
I searched every row under "Behaviours to test" that asserts a finding count
(6(c), 6(f), 7(b) through 7(h)); three of them conflict, and they conflict
with each other, not just with the Interfaces section. All three are one
fix: `:211-231` must state, per clause, whether validation stops.

- Brief `:542` (behaviour 7(c)) — "six separate mutations, one finding
  each". It lists **five** mutation kinds (a path separator, `..`, an
  upper-case letter, a leading dot, a suffix other than `.png`), so the count
  is wrong on its face. Worse, each of those mutations raises **two**
  findings under `:217-221`: the loose-vs-strict mismatch gives
  `served-e2e screenshot manifest has a malformed entry`, and the now-short
  strict count disagrees with the header's `\d+`, giving
  `served-e2e screenshot manifest count disagrees with its entries`. The row
  even acknowledges the second ("and the count mismatch it causes is
  reported") while demanding "one finding each".
- Brief `:530-533` (behaviour 6(f)) — "a manifest whose header is present but
  whose `ci/shots/<id>` names another opus produces **exactly one**
  `opus.ui.e2e`". `:213-231` specifies no stop after the directory-mismatch
  finding, so the per-entry loop at `:223` still calls
  `readContainedRegularFile(root, "<other-opus-dir>/<name>", "ci")`. In
  behaviour 6's temp officina those paths do not exist, so every entry adds
  `served-e2e screenshot <name> is unsafe or unreadable: …` — and, because
  that string matches `/unsafe|unreadable/i`, an `opus.reference` finding
  too. Not one finding; five.
- Brief `:549-551` (behaviour 7(h)) — "a header count of 3 with two `shot:`
  lines … each give **exactly one** finding" requires the per-entry loop to
  run to completion on a count mismatch. So 7(h) needs no short-circuit at
  exactly the clause where 6(f) and 7(c) need one.

**M2 — security: the rule's entry loop is unbounded and reads whole files
with no size cap.** Brief `:218-223` bounds nothing: the header's count must
equal the strict count and be at least 1, with no ceiling, and each entry is
read through `readContainedRegularFile`, which the brief itself records at
`:89` as having "no size cap" (confirmed —
`packages/commands/src/opus-model.ts:235` `readFileSync(fd)` into a `Buffer`).
`validateServed` runs inside `checkStudio`, i.e. on every `bisellium check`,
`tick`, `verify` and `/api/health` computation, for every `kind: ui` record at
`review`/`done`. The brief already names the right bound — "the manifest is
at most 64 lines" (`:193`) — but puts it only in `scripts/served-e2e.mjs`,
which is the half the rule deliberately does not trust (`:236-237`). This is
in-repo data, so it is robustness rather than privilege escalation, but it is
the one new always-on read loop the opus adds and nothing in behaviours 6 or 7
pins a ceiling. Give the rule `SHOT_MAX_ENTRIES` (64) and a per-file byte
ceiling beside `SHOT_MIN_WIDTH`/`SHOT_MIN_HEIGHT`, and a row that fires them.

**M3 — the stated guarantee that the manifest survives the pipeline's tail is
about the wrong stream.** Brief `:185-193`: "print, as the **last** lines of
the run … Nothing is printed after the manifest, and the manifest is at most
64 lines — so the pipeline's 200-line tail always keeps it whole." The
pipeline does not tail the run. `packages/pipeline/src/index.ts:89` builds
`const output = \`${r.stdout ?? ""}${r.stderr ?? ""}\`` and `:97` logs
`redact(tail(output, LOG_TAIL_LINES))` — stdout and stderr **concatenated**,
stderr last. Measured (`probe-tail.mts`): a child that writes two stderr lines
and then the manifest to stdout produces
`"shots: 1 ci/shots/W-110\nshot: board.png 1280x900 55\nnpm WARN line1\nnpm WARN line2\n"`
— the log's last line is the stderr line, and 200+ lines of stderr would evict
the manifest entirely. `served-e2e.mjs` spawns npm with `stdio: "inherit"`
(`:176`), so the whole subtree's stderr lands in that buffer.
Behaviour 5(c) (`:500-504`) pins only "stdout's last three lines", and the
Acceptance bullet at `:611-613` feeds the script's own printed manifest to the
rule rather than the pipeline's log, so nothing in the opus exercises the
concatenated tail. Measured headroom today is comfortable
(`npm --workspace @bisellium/web run build` = 14 stdout lines, **0** stderr),
so this is a mis-stated mechanism rather than an observed break — but the
sentence is load-bearing for the whole evidence chain and should either be
corrected or backed by a row that runs a manifest through
`tail(stdout ++ stderr, 200)` with stderr present.

**M4 — the forced edit to the existing ui fixtures is specified against a
helper signature that cannot carry the new header.** Brief `:358-360` tells
the builder that `servedLines`/`writeServedLog` "must emit the manifest and
write matching PNGs". `writeServedLog(root, tree)`
(`packages/cli/src/opus-model.test.ts:161`) takes **no record id**, and
`servedLines(tree)` (`:151-159`) hardcodes `evidence: ci/served.log` — one
shared log path per officina. The new header must equal `ci/shots/<record.id>`
(`:215`). The five call sites (`:1187`, `:1209`, `:1411`, `:1527`, `:1595`)
cover at least W-019 and W-023 plus three more ids, so a manifest emitted by
an id-less helper can only ever name one of them; the rest get
`served-e2e screenshot manifest names another opus's directory` and the whole
W-096 ui suite goes red for a reason the brief did not predict. Say that both
helpers gain the record id, that each root writes its own
`ci/shots/<id>/*.png`, and that the PNG fixture bytes are a shared constant —
otherwise this is a guaranteed fix round.

## SHOULD

**S1 — a measured row that no longer reproduces.** Brief `:85` (and the
Intent at `:20-22`, the fourth-thing paragraph at `:56-59`):
"`apps/web/dist/index.html` on this checkout is dated 2026-09-25". Measured
before I touched anything: `2026-10-03 08:59`. The architect's own probe
("the served console was built", verdict-1 Grounding) is what moved it. The
*mechanism* is verified and the conclusion stands — `apps/web/package.json:11`
is `playwright test --config=playwright.serve.config.ts` with no build, and
the config declares no `globalSetup` — so restate the row as the mechanism
(nothing in the served path ever builds) rather than as a timestamp that the
next probe invalidates. L-047's lesson is about the missing build step, not
about a date.

**S2 — the redact-proof invariant is incidental, not structural.** Brief
`:195-200`, residual 5 at `:691`, behaviour 5(g) at `:512`. `redact`'s
`LONG_RUN` masks runs of 32 or more `[A-Za-z0-9+/_-]`, and the brief's own
grammars admit exactly those lengths: the shot-name regex at `:218` is
`[a-z][a-z0-9-]{0,31}` — up to 32 characters, all inside the class — and the
opus-id regex at `:169`/`:213` is `[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]{0,63}`, so
`ci/shots/` plus a 23-character id already reaches 32. Measured:
`"shot: aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa.png 1280x900 10"` →
`"shot: ********.png 1280x900 10"`, and
`"shots: 2 ci/shots/aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa"` → `"shots: 2 ********"`.
A masked manifest is a permanently refused gate with no diagnostic. Cap the
name at 22 characters and the id at 22 in both the script and the rule so the
property is structural, and make 5(g) assert the boundary instead of the two
short names the stub happens to write.

**S3 — residual 6's "no new exposure" rests on an unstated assumption, and the
variables reach more commands than the residual implies.** Brief `:693-695`:
"It already ran with the repo as its cwd and could find `studio/` by itself;
no new exposure." Two gaps. (a) `localPipeline` computes `env` once above the
loop (`index.ts:75` vs `:77`), so the brief's shape at `:141-148` hands
`BISELLIUM_STUDIO_DIR` to **every** automated probatio command a manifest
declares — `tests`, `lint`, `types` and anything an adopter adds — not only
the reserved `served-e2e`. (b) "could find `studio/` by itself" is only true
while the officina lives inside the repo. `--studio <dir>` takes any path, and
the codebase treats an out-of-repo officina as a supported shape
(`packages/commands/src/lifecycle.ts:838-841` branches on `rootInside`
precisely for that case). For such an adopter the change discloses an absolute
path outside the repo to every gate command. Either scope the two variables to
the `served-e2e` gate, or state the assumption and say what happens when it
does not hold.

**S4 — the existing test for the module being changed is unmentioned.** Brief
`:324` edits `apps/web/src/lib/nav.ts`; `:369` goes out of its way to say
`apps/web/src/lib/route.test.ts` needs no edit and why. `apps/web/src/lib/nav.test.ts`
exists, is registered in root `test:suite`, and covers the same module — and
the brief adds a second nav test file, `nav-source.test.ts`, beside it without
saying so. I read it: it imports only `needsYouVisible`, so no edit is in fact
needed. Say that the way route.test.ts is said, and justify the near-duplicate
file name.

**S5 — behaviour 1(d) carries an assertion that cannot be written.** Brief
`:414`: "the file's only `#/` occurrences are inside no string literal at
all". `route.ts`'s surviving doc comment (`:2-4`) contains `/#/inbox`,
`/#/board`, `/#/seats`, `/#/officina`, and the derived map the brief specifies
at `:118-119` uses `e.href.replace(/^#/, "")`. Whatever property is intended,
name it as a concrete regex the way (d)'s first two clauses do.

**S6 — an off-by-one in a measured row.** Brief `:95`: "`--grep "computed
layout"` finds four tests". Measured at `7e019ed`: five
(`layout.spec.ts:23`, `:32`, `:44`, `:55`, `:69`). The design conclusion is
unaffected — `^` genuinely cannot anchor a playwright grep — but a measured
row should be the measurement.

**S7 — the Acceptance precondition is unsatisfiable in the main checkout and
the brief does not say so.** Brief `:577-580`: "`git status --porcelain` empty
first, so every header reads `tree:` and never `dirty:`". `.worktrees/` is
untracked and is in neither `.gitignore` (which holds `.claude/worktrees/`,
not `.worktrees/`) nor the studio's `source_excludes: [ examples/ ]`.
`isDirtyOutside` (`packages/shim/src/sourceTree.ts`) reads `git status
--porcelain` verbatim, `red` stamps `# tree: dirty:…` from it
(`packages/commands/src/lifecycle.ts:838-845`), and `next` counts a
dirty-tree red as missing (`packages/cli/src/next.ts:437-444`). The
architect's own spec log already reads `# tree: dirty:66bfec1c…`. PR 179 is
the fix. Either name it as a prerequisite, or say the reds are recorded inside
the builder's own worktree, where the main checkout's untracked directory does
not appear — but say which, because the builder will otherwise stop on red 1.

**S8 — behaviour 3(b) couples a content check to the board's state mapping,
which the brief lists as out of scope.** Brief `:451-453` asserts the set of
`[data-card-id]` values equals the id set the spec reads from `GET /api/opera`.
That holds only because every fixture state happens to map to a column:
`examples/sample-studio/opera/W-007.md` is `state: backlog`, and the brief's
own head counts put Planned at 2 (greenlit + backlog). W-129 (#174, "the board
tells the whole truth") just moved that mapping, and the brief puts the board
out of scope (`:731-732`) while `.board__backlog-footer` appears in its
"Out of scope" list. Assert the column-mapped set, or the measured literal.

## What I did not find

No vacuous "passes today" row: I traced 2(a)-(d), 3(a)-(d), 4(a), 5(a)-(b),
6(a)-(b), 7(a) and 8(a) against the sources above and each asserts something
the current tree actually does. Behaviour 3(e) does fail on the missing
`fetchActa` and not on the seeding — the seeding writes an allowance file and a
`kind: cascade` actum, neither of which touches whether `/api/acta` is
requested, and the row asserts that request independently. Behaviour 7 would
indeed fail a rule that trusted the manifest's own numbers (rows (e) and (g)
re-derive both from disk). Behaviour 5(g) would indeed fail a manifest
carrying a digest — measured. Behaviour 8(d) would indeed fail a `globalSetup`
that does not build (it asserts an `index.html` and a referenced `/assets/…`
under a temp outDir). And nothing in the opus adds, removes or restyles a
visible element: the rail gains `aria-current` and loses an unreachable
`aria-disabled`, and `Officina.tsx` gains one fetch feeding an
already-rendered `FastiStrip` — no class name, column, card or copy string
moves. The Patron's UI/UX boundary is respected.

## Round numbering

Recorded as spec round 2. The spec phase's rounds share one log sequence
(`ci/<opus>-spec-<round>.log`, `packages/commands/src/verdict.ts:264-268`,
never overwritten), and the architect's signature already holds round 1
(`studio/ci/W-110-spec-1.log`). This is the first Censor prereview of the
brief; it is advisory and writes no gate — `verdict` writes only its own log,
so the architect's `spec` gate is untouched.
